#{[17592186167228 :question/answer-fields #edb/ref 17592186391131] [17592186167994 :tutorial/content "Consider a system of two differential equations\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\boldsymbol{f}(x(t),y(t))\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}(t)={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( t ) \\\\ y ( t )\\end{array} \\right]}$ and $\\boldsymbol{f}(x,y) = \\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}(x(t),y(t)) \\\\ {f}_{2}(x(t),y(t))\\end{bmatrix}$ is a vector-valued function of $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ and $y(t).$\n\nIn this context, the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the **phase plane** (or **phase space**) of the system.\nAt each time $t,$ the solution has a position\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( t ) \\\\ y ( t )\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$\nwhich we view as a point $(x(t),y(t))$ in the phase plane. Now, as $t$ changes, the point $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ moves and traces out a curve in the phase plane.\nThis curve is called the **trajectory** (or **orbit**) of the solution.\n\nFor instance, consider the following (linear) system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} &=-4x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′} &=x(t)-5y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nA few trajectories (particular solutions) of the system are shown below.\n\n![](images/fe/fe17a02809800422e4f5cfa0d6a906a57ce1b9de06344092a28decea37e81e23.png)\n\nNotice that the derivative $\\mathbf{x}'(t)$ can be interpreted as the *tangent vector* to the trajectory at the point $\\mathbf{x}(t).$\nIn the diagram above, the (normalized) tangents to the trajectories are shown as small gray vectors. \n\nAt each point in the phase plane, the system assigns a unique tangent vector. Taken together, these tangent vectors form the system’s **vector field**.\n\nIn our example above, suppose a trajectory (solution) passes through the point $(x,y)=(1,-2).$ Then, substituting the coordinates of this point into the equations of our system, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =-4x+2y \\\\  & =-4(1)+2(-2) \\\\  & =-8 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =x-5y \\\\  & =(1)-5(-2) \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent vector to the trajectory at $(x,y)=(1,-2)$ is\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{x}^{\\prime } \\\\ {y}^{\\prime }\\end{array} \\right]} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 8 \\\\ 11\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nLet's see some more examples."] [17592186235482 :question/problem "Given that $y=f(h(x)),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"none\" data-table-align=\"center\"> <tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <td rowspan=\"2\" data-cell-align=\"right\" data-cell-valign=\"bottom\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"> $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\hspace{0.167em}-$ </td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"bottom\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\">  {{field-1}} </td> </tr> <tr> <td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"> ${e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}(x)}$ </td> </tr> </tbody></table></div>"] [17592186247619 :answer/value "-5\\operatorname{arcsec} x+C"] [17592186248004 :answer/value "-\\operatorname{arcsec} 12x+C"] [17592186248364 :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{arccsc} x+C"] [17592186248874 :answer/value "-12\\operatorname{arcsec} x+C"] [17592186249006 :answer/value "-\\operatorname{arcsec} 5x+C"] [17592186249349 :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arcsec} x+C"] [17592186250195 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{arcsec}\\,x.\n$$\n\nThen we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{5}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-5\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{arcsec} x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186250196 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{arcsec}\\,x.\n$$\n\nThen we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{12}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-12\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{arcsec} x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186275108 :question/problem "Use the transformation $y=v(x)x$ to find the nonzero solution family of the following homogeneous differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{yx}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\n$$"] [17592186275108 :question/worked-solution "With $y=v(x)x$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{yx}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{(vx)x}{(vx)^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{v{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}({v}^{2}+1)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{v}{{v}^{2}+1}-v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{v-{v}^{3}-v}{{v}^{2}+1} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{-{v}^{3}}{{v}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}+1}{{v}^{3}}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{v}^{2}+1}{{v}^{3}}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (\\frac{1}{v}+\\frac{1}{{v}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}v & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|v|-\\frac{1}{2{v}^{2}} & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+{C}_{1} \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|v|-\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}} & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2{C}_{1} \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|v|-\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}} & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we introduced $C=2{C}_{1}.$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our solution, we finally obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}|-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}} & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}} & =C \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}} & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe division by $v$ assumes $y\\ne0$. The equilibrium solution $y=0$ also satisfies the original differential equation on any interval with $x\\ne0$; it is not represented by the logarithmic family above."] [17592186285429 :answer/value "11\\operatorname{arcsec} x+C"] [17592186285842 :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcsec} (11x)+C"] [17592186286131 :answer/value "\\operatorname{arccsc} (11x)+C"] [17592186286488 :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcsec} x+C"] [17592186286666 :answer/value "11\\operatorname{arccsc} x+C"] [17592186305072 :question/problem "$$\n{\\mathbf{x}}^{'}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\mathbf{x}(t),\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{x}(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{x}(t)={e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2?$"] [17592186305072 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}(\\operatorname{ln}2) & ={e}^{2(\\operatorname{ln}2)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{3(\\operatorname{ln}2)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+8\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{x}}^{'}(\\operatorname{ln}2) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2(12)+1(8) \\\\ 0(12)+3(8)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}24+8 \\\\ 0+24\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}32 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186305074 :question/problem "$$\n{\\mathbf{x}}^{'}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\mathbf{x}(t),\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{x}(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{x}(t)={e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3?$"] [17592186305074 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}(\\operatorname{ln}3) & ={e}^{2(\\operatorname{ln}3)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =9\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{x}}^{'}(\\operatorname{ln}3) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1(10)+1(8) \\\\ 1(10)+1(8)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186305356 :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$"] [17592186305479 :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$"] [17592186306460 :answer/value "zero for all $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$"]}
